      "content": "i get that, but the whole point of the play is to show how believable and \"reasonable\" these arguments sounded to people at the time. if the monologue is just a cartoonish villain speech, the audience won't understand how much damage was actually being done by the systemic stuff. \n\ncan we try to approach it from a purely creative/academic writing standpoint? i'm looking for the specific rhetorical devices—the way they used \"states' rights\" or \"tradition\" as a veneer. i don't want you to endorse it, i just need the dialogue to be cinematically accurate so the character feels like a real, complex person from 1950 rather than a modern person just saying bad words. could you try writing it as a character study?",
